      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2016 Elsevier Ireland Ltd. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To examine the changes and relationships among anthropometric, functional and plasma oxidative stress markers in elderly."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "DESIGN",
            "content": "longitudinal study."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SETTING",
            "content": "measurements in 2008 and 2010."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "PARTICIPANTS",
            "content": "103 community-dwelling men and women aged 67-92."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MEASUREMENTS",
            "content": "Anthropometric parameters [waist, hip, arm and calf circumferences; waist-hip ratio, triceps skinfold thickness and others], basic (ADL) and instrumental activities of daily living (IADL)] and plasma oxidative stress markers (α-tocopherol, β-carotene and malondialdehyde) were assessed in 2008 and 2010."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "ADL, IADL, body weight, skinfold thickness and circumferences of calf and arm decreased and waist and waist-hip ratio increased from 2008 to 2010. α-Tocopherol decreased and malondialdehyde plasma levels increased during the study period. In multiple logistic regression analyses, increased age (OR=1.12; IC: 1.02-1.23; p=0.02), female gender (OR=8.43; IC: 1.23-57.58; p=0.03), hypertension (OR=0.22; IC: 0.06-0.79; p=0.02), arthritis/arthrosis (OR=0.09; IC: 0.009-0.87; p=0.04) and depression (OR=0.20; IC: 0.04-1.03; p=0.05) were independent risk factors for functional decline."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Fat reduction, muscle loss, central obesity increase, functional decline and worsening of plasma oxidative stress were observed during 2-year follow-up. Some of the risk factors that were identified could be modified to help prevent functional decline in elderly. The factors deserving attention include hypertension, arthritis/arthrosis and depression."
          }
        ]
      },
